[{"id": "question 1185", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "In TS 38.331 the IE SL-CBR-RangeConfigList has maxCBR-Config-r16=8, capping the number of CBR range configurations for sidelink congestion control at 8.", "source": "TS 38.331 V17.6.0 SL-CBR-CommonTxConfigList ASN.1 (itecspec spec repo); own knowledge"}, {"id": "question 1196", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "TS 23.540 is '5G System: Technical realization of Service Based Short Message Service' (SMS), not TSN integration. The 3GPP TS that defines the 5G System integration into a TSN DN (architecture + procedures) is TS 23.501/TS 23.502. IEEE 802.1Q is the IEEE TSN standard, but the question is tagged [3GPP Release 18] so TS 23.502 is the correct 3GPP standard.", "source": "3GPP TS series list (23-series) confirming TS 23.540 = SMS; ITU presentation '5G/5G-Advanced System for Supporting Deterministic Networking and Time-Sensitive Networks' confirming TSN integration in 23.501/23.502"}, {"id": "question 1226", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "Per TS 23.288 (and Release 18 extension), upon receiving an enabling request for analytics information exposure from a UE, the SMF performs an authorization check (against SM subscription data from UDM/local config) and then includes an Accept or Reject (NACK) in the PDU Session Establishment Accept message to the UE.", "source": "WO/2023/172094 patent description of TS 23.288 procedure; US 20240283849 description"}, {"id": "question 1230", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "In IEEE 802.11 (802.11af / TVWS), RLSS is defined as 'Registered Location Secure Server' — a server that stores the geographic location and operating parameters for a small number of BSSs.", "source": "IEEE 802.11af / IEEE 11-11-1421-01-00af doc; arXiv 1712.05003 describing IEEE 802.11af architecture"}, {"id": "question 1241", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 5, "evidence": "In Release 17, the TRS availability indication field (in DCI format 1_0 / 2_7) is a bitmap where each bit corresponds to a TRS resource set identified by indBitID; a '1' value indicates that the associated TRS resource set is present/available.", "source": "TS 38.331 V17 SIB17 field descriptions (indBitID); US patent US20260143503 explaining TRS availability indication in DCI"}, {"id": "question 1244", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 28.105 (V17.3.0), an 'AI decision entity' is defined as 'an entity that applies a non-ML based logic to make AI decisions, which can be managed as a single composite entity'.", "source": "ETSI TS 128 105 V17.3.0 terminology (antpedia standard page); CN117716674A / WO2023114017A1 reproducing TS 28.105 definitions"}, {"id": "question 1258", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "TS 26.348 defines the xMB API at the xMB reference point between the BM-SC and the content provider; its purpose is to allow the content provider to control MBMS delivery procedures (service announcement, session start/stop, bearer activation, etc.).", "source": "3GPP TS 26.348 'Northbound Application Programming Interface (API) for MBMS at the xMB reference point' (3GPP TSG-S4 reference in S4-221299); TS 26.512 procedures using xMB"}, {"id": "question 1272", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "Per IEEE 802.11 (802.11af) architecture description: 'In some regulatory domains a Registered Location Secure Server can provide to GDBs the current channel use information of all the BSSs and IBSSs that communicate with it.'", "source": "IEEE 802.11 document 11-11-1421-01-00af; ResearchGate 'WiFi/WhiteFi Handover' paper"}, {"id": "question 1287", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "No NGAP message named 'INITIAL UL RRC MESSAGE TRANSFER' exists in TS 38.413 (the listed NGAP messages are INITIAL UE MESSAGE, DL RRC MESSAGE TRANSFER, UL RRC MESSAGE TRANSFER). 'INITIAL UL RRC MESSAGE TRANSFER' exists in F1AP (TS 38.473) but it carries the initial RRC message from the UE (RRCSetupRequest), not the low-layer configuration. The RRC messages containing the lower-layer configuration (CellGroupConfig / radioBearerConfig) are carried via DL RRC MESSAGE TRANSFER.", "source": "TS 38.413 NGAP message list; TS 38.473 F1AP message list; own knowledge"}, {"id": "question 1295", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "IEEE 802.15.4 explicitly states: 'If a beacon frame is received when macAutoRequest is set to FALSE, each recorded PAN descriptor is sent to the next higher layer in a separate MLME-BEACON-NOTIFY.indication primitive.'", "source": "IEEE 802.15.4-2003 §7.5.2.1.2 (mentor.ieee.org 15-06-0192-03-004a document) and 802.15.4 standard text"}, {"id": "question 1299", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Multi-server MBMS bearer coordination (Rel-15 MBMS_Mcservices) is one of the MBMS enhancements for MC services and concerns coordinating MBMS bearers between multiple MC service servers. The marked option describes 'a single MC service server' managing all MBMS media transmission, which contradicts the 'multi-server' framing of the feature; the listed options do not describe the actual purpose (inter-MC-server coordination to avoid duplicate bearers).", "source": "3GPP CCW 2018 presentation listing Rel-15 MBMS_Mcservices enhancements; TS 23.379 / TS 23.468 GCS AS architecture"}, {"id": "question 1309", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "In 3GPP UE radio specs (e.g., TS 38.101-1), a 'mandatory' requirement is one specified by 3GPP as mandatory for the UE; e.g., 'The minimum performance requirements in Clause 5 are mandatory for UE supporting NR operation'. It is not the same as a regulatory requirement of a country/region.", "source": "TS 38.101-1/3/4 §4.2 and §5.1.1.1 (3GPP/ARIB/ETSI published PDFs); own reasoning"}, {"id": "question 1330", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "VLANs (defined in IEEE 802.1Q; 802.3 is the Ethernet standard) work by creating separate broadcast domains; every VLAN limits broadcast traffic to its own members, so the statement 'All VLANs limit the problem of broadcast messages' is true.", "source": "own knowledge of IEEE 802.1Q VLAN fundamentals"}, {"id": "question 1355", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per the TR 21.914 S8 Home Routing Architecture summary: 'Most of the CT groups work [in V8-CT] was to add the new procedure to fetch and provide necessary information to establish emergency call.' The V8-CT WI thus focuses on fetching and providing necessary information for emergency call (in the S8HR VoLTE roaming context).", "source": "whatthespec.net entries for V8-CT WIs (730024/730025); TR 21.914 'S8 Home Routing Architecture for VoLTE' (itecspec)"}, {"id": "question 1380", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 23.501 §5.21.2.3 (Procedure for AMF Auto-recovery) states that '5G-AN marks this AMF as failed and not consider the AMF for selection until explicitly notified' as one of the actions when the 5G-AN detects an AMF failure (along with OAM deregistering from NRF and releasing NGAP UE-TNLA-bindings).", "source": "TS 23.501 V18.x §5.21.2.3 (itecspec 23.501/s/5.21.2.3); US 11418979 patent text"}, {"id": "question 1411", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "For NWDAF containing MTLF, when using untrusted AF(s) as data sources, the trustworthiness/rating of the untrusted AF(s) is considered (in addition to general ML model accuracy which applies regardless of AF trust). The marked answer reflects the AF-rating aspect specific to untrusted AFs.", "source": "TS 29.520 V18 Nnwdaf_MLModelProvision overview + MLModelMetric (ACCURACY); 3GPP 'Network Automation Enablers in 5GS' page describing MTLF/AnLF and data sources"}, {"id": "question 1426", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "TS 22.104 (Service requirements for cyber-physical control applications / tactile & multi-modal communication) requires the 5G system to support synchronization between different media components (e.g., audio, video, haptic) for tactile and multi-modal communication services.", "source": "TS 22.104 (3GPP TS 22.104 v17.0/v18.4 cyber-physical control applications); own knowledge of service requirements"}, {"id": "question 1445", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per TS 23.548 and TS 29.556 (EASDF in 5GC): the SMF and other 5GC NFs interact with the EASDF for EASDF discovery and selection, e.g. The SMF selects an EASDF instance serving the PDU session.", "source": "TS 23.548 (5G System Enhancements for Edge Computing); 3GPP 'Edge computing in CT protocols' page; 3gpp-explorer EASDF glossary"}]